[{'id': 'MFA-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The application shall require multifactor authentication (MFA) for all user logins, using at least one factor from the 'Something You Know', 'Something You Have', or 'Something You Are' categories.", 'rationale': "MFA is the best defense against password-related attacks such as brute-force and credential stuffing, as demonstrated by Microsoft's analysis showing it would have stopped 99.9% of account compromises.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to log in to the application;
WHEN the user provides a valid username and password;
THEN the system shall prompt for a second authentication factor (e.g., TOTP code) and only allow access upon successful verification.'}, {'id': 'MFA-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall require MFA for any action that modifies user account settings, including changing passwords, email addresses, or security questions.', 'rationale': 'Sensitive actions that alter user data are high-risk and should be protected by MFA to prevent unauthorized changes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to change their password or email address;
WHEN the user has not completed MFA;
THEN the system shall require MFA before allowing the action to proceed and only permit it after successful MFA verification.'}, {'id': 'MFA-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall enforce a time-to-live (TTL) of no more than 30 seconds for one-time passwords (OTPs) generated during authentication.', 'rationale': 'OTPs must be short-lived to prevent interception and reuse, reducing the risk of account compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an OTP is generated during login;
WHEN the user attempts to use it after 30 seconds;
THEN the system shall reject the request and prompt for a new OTP.'}, {'id': 'MFA-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall not store or log OTP values in plaintext or in any form that allows reconstruction of the original value.', 'rationale': 'Improper handling of OTPs can lead to user account compromise, as OTPs are authentication secrets and must be treated like passwords.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an OTP is generated;
WHEN it is stored or logged;
THEN the system shall ensure that the value is not stored in plaintext and is not logged in any form that could allow reconstruction.'}, {'id': 'MFA-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall provide a user-friendly interface to enable and manage MFA via TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password) tokens.', 'rationale': 'Users must be able to easily configure MFA, especially if they are not technically proficient, to ensure adoption and usability.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user is on the application login page;
WHEN they select 'Enable MFA';
THEN they shall be guided through a step-by-step process to set up a TOTP token using a mobile app, with clear instructions and error messages."}]

Actors/Assets: Two network devices (e.g., firewalls or routers) establishing an IPsec tunnel; the data payload transmitted over the tunnel.

Key Functional Concerns: Encryption and data origin authentication of IPsec ESP payloads using AES-GCM mode; construction of ESP payload data with an 8-octet IV and ciphertext;

Non-Functional Concerns: Performance at high data rates (e.g., 10+ Gbps); efficiency in both hardware and software implementations;

Security Concerns: Prevention of nonce reuse; integrity and confidentiality of data; protection against replay and tampering;

Assumptions: The devices support IPsec ESP and AES-GCM; the key management system (e.g., IKE) is operational and configured to prevent nonce/key reuse;

Missing Information: Specific network topology, device types, data types being transmitted, key lifecycle management, and operational environment (e.g., public vs private network).

[{'id': 'KR-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall use symmetric-key algorithms to provide confidentiality for data at rest and in transit, with key lengths of at least 128 bits as specified in NIST SP 800-57 Part 1.', 'rationale': 'Symmetric-key algorithms are efficient and suitable for data encryption at rest and in transit. A 128-bit key provides sufficient strength against current and foreseeable attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN user data is stored locally or transmitted via HTTPS;
WHEN the data is encrypted using a symmetric key;
THEN the data shall be unreadable without the correct key, and the key shall not be disclosed to unauthorized entities.'}, {'id': 'KR-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall use asymmetric-key algorithms to authenticate end devices and generate digital signatures, with key pairs of at least 2048 bits as specified in NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 and the NSA Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite 2.0.', 'rationale': 'Asymmetric-key algorithms provide secure device authentication and digital signatures. A 2048-bit key pair offers strong resistance to brute-force and factorization attacks, especially in the context of quantum-resistant future threats.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a device attempts to connect to the application;
WHEN the device presents a valid certificate or public key;
THEN the application shall verify the device's identity using asymmetric key authentication and reject unauthorized devices."}, {'id': 'KR-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall implement HMACs using a cryptographic hash function (e.g., SHA-256) to provide data origin authentication and integrity protection for transmitted data, as defined in FIPS 198.', 'rationale': 'HMACs provide a secure way to verify data origin and integrity without requiring public-key infrastructure. SHA-256 is a widely approved and secure hash function per FIPS 180.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN data is transmitted from the device to the server;
WHEN the data is signed with an HMAC using a shared symmetric key;
THEN the server shall validate the HMAC and reject any data that fails integrity checks.'}, {'id': 'KR-04', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'All cryptographic keys shall be generated using cryptographically secure random number generators (CSPRNGs) as defined in NIST SP 800-90A, and stored in a secure, isolated environment (e.g., device secure element or OS keychain).', 'rationale': 'Keys must be generated with true randomness to prevent predictability. Secure storage prevents exposure during device operation or during memory access.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key is generated for data encryption;
WHEN the key is generated using a CSPRNG;
THEN the key shall not be reproducible or predictable, and it shall be stored in a secure, non-volatile, isolated storage mechanism.'}, {'id': 'KR-05', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall ensure that key material is zeroized when no longer needed, and key compromise shall be handled through a defined recovery or destruction process as per key management best practices.', 'rationale': 'Key zeroization prevents residual data exposure. A defined process ensures that compromised keys are not reused or retained, reducing risk of re-use attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key is no longer required for use;
WHEN the key is no longer used;
THEN the key material shall be zeroized, and the key shall be removed from all memory and storage.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall use the ACME protocol (RFC 8555) to register and manage certificate requests.', 'rationale': "ACME is the standardized protocol for automated certificate issuance and is widely adopted by CAs such as Let's Encrypt and Sectigo.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain owner submits a certificate request via the web interface;
WHEN the system performs DNS-based validation;
THEN it shall successfully issue a certificate using the ACME protocol and return a valid certificate to the user.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The system shall validate domain ownership using DNS TXT records with a specific format as defined in RFC 8555 and RFC 6844.', 'rationale': 'DNS TXT records provide a standardized, secure, and widely supported method for domain ownership validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain owner provides a domain name;
WHEN the system attempts to validate ownership by creating a DNS TXT record;
THEN the record must be successfully created and verified within 10 minutes of issuance.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'All issued certificates shall be signed using RSA-2048 or stronger cryptographic algorithms as specified in RFC 7515 and RFC 7518.', 'rationale': 'Strong key sizes are required to ensure long-term security against cryptographic attacks, as per CA/Browser Forum baseline requirements.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate is issued;
WHEN the certificate is validated;
THEN the public key shall be at least 2048 bits in length and use RSA or ECDSA as specified in RFC 7515 and RFC 7518.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'requirement': 'The system shall support certificate renewal automatically, with a minimum of 30 days before expiration, using the ACME protocol.', 'rationale': 'Automated renewal prevents certificate expiration and maintains continuous service availability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate is issued with a 90-day validity period;
WHEN the system detects that 30 days remain before expiration;
THEN it shall initiate a renewal request via ACME and successfully obtain a new certificate.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall avoid direct execution of OS commands using user-supplied input. Instead, it shall use built-in library functions (e.g., java.net.URLConnection in Java) to fetch data from a URL, to prevent OS command injection.', 'rationale': 'Direct OS command execution (e.g., system(), Runtime.exec) is vulnerable to command injection attacks. Using built-in libraries eliminates the risk of executing unintended commands via user input.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a URL via the API;
WHEN the application uses a built-in library function to fetch data;
THEN no OS command is executed, and no shell metacharacters are interpreted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If direct OS command execution is unavoidable, the application shall escape user input using OS-specific escaping functions (e.g., escapeshellarg in PHP, or ProcessBuilder in Java) to prevent argument injection.', 'rationale': 'Without proper escaping, user input containing shell metacharacters (e.g., & | ;) can modify the command execution flow, leading to unintended command execution or information disclosure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a URL containing shell metacharacters (e.g., & | ;);
WHEN the input is passed to a system command;
THEN the input is properly escaped so that metacharacters are not interpreted as command operators.'}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall validate user input against an allowlist of allowed characters and length constraints. The input shall only contain lowercase letters, numbers, and valid URL syntax, with a maximum length of 100 characters.', 'rationale': 'Allowlist validation prevents injection of malicious metacharacters (e.g., $ > < ` \) that could be used to alter command execution or exploit system vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a URL;
WHEN the input contains metacharacters outside the allowlist (e.g., $, |, &) or exceeds 100 characters;
THEN the request shall be rejected with a 400 Bad Request error and no command execution shall occur.'}, {'id': 'SEC-04', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall run with the minimum required privileges to perform its tasks, and shall not execute commands with elevated system privileges.', 'rationale': 'Running with minimal privileges limits the potential damage from any compromised execution, reducing the risk of privilege escalation and system compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application is running in a production environment;
WHEN a command injection vulnerability is exploited;
THEN the attacker shall not be able to execute commands with elevated privileges or access system-level resources.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-01', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': "The API shall accept a POST request with a JSON body containing a 'url' field and return the fetched data in a JSON response with a 200 OK status code.", 'rationale': 'This defines the expected input and output format for the API endpoint to ensure consistent and predictable behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a valid URL is submitted in a POST request;
WHEN the API processes the request;
THEN it returns a JSON response containing the fetched data with a 200 OK status code.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The web application shall enforce a same-origin policy such that any script injected into an HTML document shall only have authority over objects that the script can designate, and shall not have access to sensitive user data such as medical records.', 'rationale': 'This prevents attackers from gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data through cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks by limiting script authority to designated objects only.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user uploads a medical record to the API AND an attacker injects a script into an HTML document hosted by a third party;
WHEN the script runs in the browser;
THEN the script shall not access or retrieve any user medical records, and the origin authority shall be restricted to only the objects the script can designate.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': "The application shall use the 'Origin' header in HTTP requests to define the origin of the request and shall validate that the origin matches the expected domain structure.", 'rationale': "The 'Origin' header is standardized in HTTP and is used to enforce origin-based security boundaries, preventing unauthorized access between domains.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request from a client to the API;
WHEN the request includes an 'Origin' header;
THEN the server shall validate the header and reject any request from a domain that does not match the expected origin authority."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The application shall support IDNA2008 (RFC 5890) for internationalized domain name handling and shall not allow domain name mappings that result in unintended security boundary changes.', 'rationale': 'Using IDNA2008 ensures consistent and secure domain name representation, avoiding potential security boundary erosion that could occur when migrating from IDNA2003 to IDNA2008.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain name with U+00DF or other international characters;
WHEN the domain is resolved by the user agent;
THEN the domain name shall be mapped using IDNA2008, and no security boundary shall be created or destroyed as a result of the mapping.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'Access to object storage must be managed through Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies, with no direct access to storage via public URLs or hardcoded credentials.', 'rationale': "Using IAM policies ensures indirect access and reduces the risk of credential exposure and unauthorized access. This aligns with the 'Secure Object Storage' section of the cheat sheet, which emphasizes the risks of direct access and credential loss.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to access object storage via a direct URL or hardcoded credential;
WHEN the system enforces IAM policies;
THEN access is denied or logged, and no direct access is granted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'All signed URLs for file access must be cryptographically generated and expire after a configurable time period (e.g., 15 minutes), and must not allow anonymous access.', 'rationale': 'Signed URLs provide secure, temporary access to files, reducing the risk of long-term exposure. The cheat sheet notes that custom, dynamic URL generation can introduce code injection risks, so expiration and access controls are critical.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user requests a signed URL for a file;
WHEN the URL is generated with a time-bound signature and no anonymous access;
THEN the file can only be accessed within the time window and only by authenticated users.'}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'Public object storage must not be used for any user data or files; all data must be stored in private object storage within a VPC.', 'rationale': 'Public object storage exposes data to the public internet and violates privacy and security principles. The cheat sheet explicitly states this method is not advisable for sensitive data or any data stored for any period of time.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a file is uploaded to the system;
WHEN the system attempts to store it in public object storage;
THEN the operation is rejected and the file is stored in a private, VPC-protected object storage.'}, {'id': 'NET-01', 'type': 'non-functional', 'description': 'The application must deploy in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with at least one public subnet and one private subnet.', 'rationale': 'VPCs and subnet segmentation provide network isolation, which is a foundational security practice. The cheat sheet recommends VPCs for separating applications and components with distinct access needs.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application is deployed;
WHEN the system is configured with a VPC;
THEN it has at least one public subnet for front-end services and one private subnet for backend services and databases.'}, {'id': 'NET-02', 'type': 'non-functional', 'description': 'All components in the private subnet must not have direct internet access and must only communicate via secure, internal network routing.', 'rationale': 'Private subnets are recommended for sensitive components like databases and backend servers to prevent direct exposure to the internet.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a backend server is deployed in a private subnet;
WHEN a request is made to access it;
THEN the request is routed through the VPC and no direct internet access is allowed.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The IDPS shall perform network traffic analysis at the IP and transport layers to detect suspicious patterns and known attack signatures.', 'rationale': 'Supports detection of common threats such as port scans, TCP reset attacks, and protocol misuse based on established IDPS capabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN network traffic is captured at the IP and transport layers;
WHEN a known attack signature or suspicious pattern is detected;
THEN the IDPS shall generate an alert and initiate a blocking action within 10 seconds.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The IDPS shall support both signature-based and network behavior analysis (NBA) detection methods.', 'rationale': 'Signature-based detection provides known threat coverage; NBA enables detection of novel or unknown threats based on behavioral anomalies.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network session with abnormal traffic patterns;
WHEN the IDPS detects a deviation from baseline behavior;
THEN it shall trigger a warning and initiate a review process within 5 minutes.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'requirement': 'The IDPS shall maintain logs of all detection events, actions taken, and system performance metrics for at least 90 days.', 'rationale': 'Logging is essential for incident response, forensic analysis, and compliance with logging and monitoring standards.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a detection event occurs;
WHEN the IDPS processes the event;
THEN a log entry shall be generated and stored in a secure, accessible location for at least 90 days.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The IDPS shall support integration with a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system via syslog or API for centralized monitoring and alerting.', 'rationale': 'Centralized monitoring improves visibility and enables correlation of events across multiple systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a detection event occurs;
WHEN the IDPS detects a security policy violation;
THEN it shall send a syslog message to the SIEM system within 2 seconds.'}]

[{'id': 'ASVS-V1.1', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All user inputs must be validated and sanitized to prevent injection attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS).', 'rationale': 'Injection attacks are common in web applications and can lead to unauthorized data access or system compromise. This requirement aligns with ASVS V1.1 on encoding and sanitization.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a file name or description via the web interface;
WHEN the input is processed;
THEN it shall be validated and sanitized to remove or escape any characters that could be used in injection attacks, and no malicious payloads shall be executed.'}, {'id': 'ASVS-V2.2', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All inputs must undergo strict validation to ensure they conform to expected formats and data types, including file type, size, and content structure.', 'rationale': 'Input validation is a foundational defense against many security vulnerabilities, including malformed data and unintended operations. This requirement is supported by ASVS V2.2 on input validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user uploads a file;
WHEN the file is received;
THEN the system shall validate the file type, size, and content against defined policies, and reject any input that does not meet the criteria with a clear error message.'}, {'id': 'ASVS-V2.3', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Business logic must be explicitly documented and implemented to prevent unauthorized access or manipulation of file sharing operations.', 'rationale': 'Business logic flaws can allow attackers to bypass access controls or perform unintended actions. ASVS V2.3 emphasizes the need for secure business logic design and documentation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to share a file with another user;
WHEN the operation is performed;
THEN the system shall enforce access controls based on user roles and permissions, and no unauthorized sharing or deletion shall occur.'}, {'id': 'ASVS-V1.5', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All deserialization operations must be performed safely to prevent remote code execution via malicious payloads.', 'rationale': 'Deserialization is a common attack vector in web applications. ASVS V1.5 provides guidance on safe deserialization practices to prevent vulnerabilities like deserialization attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a file is received in a serialized format;
WHEN it is deserialized;
THEN the system shall validate the structure and content, and reject any payload that contains unexpected or untrusted data.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The legacy payroll application shall only be accessible to users with the minimum required privileges based on the principle of least privilege.', 'rationale': 'Legacy applications are inherently high-risk due to outdated technology and lack of vendor support. Applying least privilege reduces the attack surface and limits potential damage from compromised access.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to access the payroll application;
WHEN the user does not have a role defined as necessary for payroll processing;
THEN access shall be denied with a clear error message, and logging shall record the failed access attempt.'}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The organization shall maintain a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) for the legacy payroll application that lists all third-party dependencies and their versions.', 'rationale': 'An SBOM enables visibility into third-party components that may contain known vulnerabilities, which is critical for managing risks in legacy systems with no vendor support.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the legacy payroll application;
WHEN the SBOM is generated and maintained;
THEN it shall be accessible to the security team and updated at least quarterly, and any known vulnerabilities in dependencies shall be documented and tracked.'}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The legacy payroll application shall be restricted to operate only on a dedicated, isolated network segment with no direct access to the internal corporate network or the internet.', 'rationale': 'Isolating the application reduces the risk of lateral movement by attackers and limits exposure to external threats and internal breaches.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempting to access the application from outside the dedicated segment;
WHEN the network firewall is configured to block all external traffic;
THEN access shall be denied, and logs shall show the blocked attempt.'}, {'id': 'SEC-04', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The organization shall conduct a formal risk assessment using the NIST Risk Management Framework to evaluate the potential impact of a compromise of the payroll application.', 'rationale': 'A structured risk assessment helps quantify the business impact of a breach and informs prioritization of security controls for legacy applications.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a scenario where the payroll data is compromised;
WHEN the NIST RMF assessment is completed;
THEN the assessment shall identify the likelihood and impact of such an event, and the results shall be reviewed by the security and compliance teams.'}]

[{'id': 'MFA-01', 'requirement': 'Authentication shall require at least two factors: something the user knows (e.g., password) and something the user has (e.g., TOTP or U2F token).', 'rationale': 'Per NIST SP 800-63, multifactor authentication is required for high-assurance systems. Using two factors significantly reduces the risk of account compromise compared to passwords alone.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to log in to an internal web application;
WHEN they enter a valid password and a valid one-time code from a TOTP or U2F token;
THEN authentication shall succeed and no further factors shall be required.'}, {'id': 'MFA-02', 'requirement': 'The system shall not use security questions as an authentication factor.', 'rationale': 'NIST SP 800-63 explicitly disallows security questions as an acceptable authentication factor due to their susceptibility to guessing and social media exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to authenticate via security questions;
WHEN the system prompts for a security question;
THEN the system shall reject the authentication attempt and provide an error message indicating that security questions are not supported.'}, {'id': 'MFA-03', 'requirement': 'The authentication system shall be resistant to phishing attacks, particularly for TOTP and U2F tokens.', 'rationale': 'Phishing remains a major threat; U2F tokens are resistant to phishing because private keys remain on the device, while TOTP codes are short-lived and can be invalidated if the device is compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempts to trick a user into entering a TOTP code from a fake application;
WHEN the user enters the code;
THEN the system shall reject the authentication attempt and log the event as a potential phishing attempt.'}, {'id': 'MFA-04', 'requirement': 'If a user loses their TOTP or U2F token, a new token must be issued or a new authenticator app must be configured without requiring physical delivery of a new token.', 'rationale': 'Software-based TOTP allows for easier recovery and configuration, reducing administrative overhead and user disruption compared to hardware tokens.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user reports losing their TOTP app or hardware token;
WHEN the user requests a new authenticator;
THEN the system shall allow the user to reconfigure a new TOTP app on their mobile device or issue a new U2F token via secure administrative channel, without requiring physical delivery.'}]

[{'id': 'KR-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Cryptographic keys shall be generated within a cryptographic module that is FIPS 140-2 or FIPS 140-3 compliant, with the random bit generator implemented within the same module.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that key generation occurs in a secure environment with cryptographically strong randomness, as specified in NIST SP 800-133.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key generation module with FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliance;
WHEN a key is generated;
THEN the random value used in generation must be produced by a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliant random bit generator within the same module.'}, {'id': 'KR-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Each cryptographic key shall be used for only one purpose (e.g., encryption, digital signatures, key wrapping), and key strength shall be determined based on the expected duration of data protection and adversary sophistication.', 'rationale': 'Prevents key reuse across functions, which could weaken security or allow broader damage if compromised, as per NIST guidance on key usage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key intended for use in digital signatures;
WHEN it is used in an encryption function;
THEN a security violation occurs and the system shall reject such usage.'}, {'id': 'KR-03', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': "Keys stored in memory shall be split into components and updated frequently to prevent 'burning in' and loss of protection.", 'rationale': 'Mitigates the risk of long-term memory exposure leading to key compromise, as outlined in NIST SP 800-57.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key stored in memory for more than 30 minutes;
WHEN the key is accessed;
THEN at least one component of the key shall be rotated or updated to prevent static exposure.'}, {'id': 'KR-04', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All key distribution shall occur via secure channels and keys shall be used within FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliant cryptographic modules.', 'rationale': 'Ensures secure transport and protected execution environments for keys, as per NIST SP 800-133.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key to be distributed;
WHEN transmitted over a network;
THEN the transmission must occur over an encrypted channel and the key must be used in a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliant module.'}]

[{'id': 'FW-SEC-001', 'requirement': 'Firewall policies shall deny all incoming TCP and UDP traffic by default, and only permit specific traffic based on defined application or service rules.', 'rationale': 'Following the principle of least privilege and defense in depth, deny-by-default policies reduce the attack surface for incoming traffic.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a firewall policy configuration for incoming traffic;
WHEN a packet is received on an unlisted port or protocol;
THEN it shall be dropped or logged, and no traffic shall be allowed to pass.'}, {'id': 'FW-SEC-002', 'requirement': 'Firewall policies shall allow only specific ICMPv6 message types and codes for incoming and outgoing traffic, as defined in RFC 4890, and only when necessary for operational functions such as PMTU discovery or neighbor discovery.', 'rationale': 'ICMPv6 is essential for IPv6 operation, but uncontrolled ICMP traffic can be used for reconnaissance or denial-of-service attacks; strict filtering ensures security without disrupting core functionality.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with an ICMPv6 type and code;
WHEN the type and code are not in the approved list;
THEN the packet shall be dropped or logged, and no response shall be sent.'}, {'id': 'FW-SEC-003', 'requirement': 'For IPsec traffic, firewalls shall block ESP and AH protocols except for traffic destined to or originating from designated IPsec gateways on the internal network.', 'rationale': 'Allowing IPsec traffic to all endpoints increases exposure to attacks; limiting access to gateways ensures only authorized devices can establish secure tunnels.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an IPsec ESP or AH packet from a non-designated IP address;
WHEN the packet is received at the firewall;
THEN it shall be dropped or logged, and no forwarding shall occur.'}, {'id': 'FW-SEC-004', 'requirement': 'Inbound application firewalls or proxies shall be implemented in front of any server that lacks sufficient built-in security features to defend against application-specific attacks.', 'rationale': 'Application-level filtering provides an additional layer of defense, preventing malicious payloads from reaching servers and reducing server load and exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server exposed to external traffic;
WHEN an application firewall or proxy is deployed in front;
THEN all incoming application traffic shall be validated before reaching the server, and malicious payloads shall be blocked or quarantined.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The web interface shall use TLS 1.2 or higher to encrypt all data in transit between the client and the server.', 'rationale': 'Unencrypted communication exposes sensor configuration data and commands to eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks, especially in untrusted network segments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to access the web interface over an unsecured network;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN the client shall be redirected to a secure HTTPS connection or receive an error indicating the connection is not secure.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All user access to the sensor configuration interface shall be authenticated using strong credentials (e.g., username and password) and verified through role-based access control (RBAC).', 'rationale': 'Without authentication, adversaries could impersonate engineers and modify sensor parameters, leading to physical process manipulation or denial of control.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an unauthorized user attempts to access the configuration interface;
WHEN the request is processed;
THEN the system shall reject the request and return a 401 Unauthorized response with no exposure of sensitive data.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All sensor configuration commands shall be transmitted with integrity checks to detect unauthorized modification or tampering during transmission.', 'rationale': 'Without integrity checks, adversaries could modify configuration data in transit and undetectably alter sensor behavior, leading to unsafe process operations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration command is sent from the client to the server;
WHEN the command is intercepted and modified;
THEN the server shall detect the change and reject the command with a clear error message.'}, {'id': 'NET-001', 'category': 'Network Security', 'requirement': 'Wireless access points serving sensor devices shall enforce strong mutual authentication between clients and access points to prevent rogue access point connections.', 'rationale': 'Unauthorized wireless access allows adversaries to connect to sensor networks and manipulate physical processes directly.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a rogue access point is deployed in the OT environment;
WHEN a client attempts to connect to it;
THEN the access point shall reject the connection and log the event.'}, {'id': 'NET-002', 'category': 'Network Security', 'requirement': 'All data transmitted between wireless clients and access points shall be protected using strong encryption (e.g., WPA3 or AES-256) to prevent unauthorized access to unencrypted data.', 'rationale': 'Unencrypted data in wireless traffic can be intercepted and used to manipulate sensor parameters or exfiltrate sensitive operational data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a wireless client transmits sensor configuration data;
